description:
Building under construction in Lansing, Michigan.

Text on reverse of card: &quot;G. W. Wood &amp; Co., Portrait and Landscape Photographers, Cor. Washington and Michigan Aves., Lansing, Mich.&quot;

Written on reverse of card: &quot;This is a sample of the pictures in a double glass framed viewer I saved as a relic the Kings had, but house burned and destroyed, can't say where I got it from as I am close to 89 years old now. T. O. D. 'The Old Devil' a nickname our two grandmas had for me. Emma E.&quot;

Date not identified.

Plymouth Congregational Church, 221 West Allegan Street, Lansing, Michigan.

On front of card: &quot;Views In Lansing &amp; Vicinity. J. H. Scotford, Photo, Lansing, Mich.&quot;

Date not identified.

The Lansing High School, constructed in 1875 to replace the original wooden building built in 1868.. Architects were Meyers and Gillette, and construction was done by contractor H. D. Mason. The school was located at 401 North Capitol Avenue.

On reverse of card: &quot;B. F. Hall, Portrait and Landscape Photographer, Lansing, Mich.&quot; &quot;Stereoscopic Views of Lansing and vicinity on hand and taken to order.&quot;

Date not identified.

A view of the recently constructed Michigan State Capitol building. View is to the west at the main facade. Lumber or brush and stacks of pipes are visible.

On front of card: &quot;J. H. Scotford. Lansing, Mich.&quot; &quot;Stereoscopic Views of the New Capitol, &amp; Lansing and Vicinity.&quot;

Date not identified, circa 1879.

A view of the recently constructed Michigan State Capitol building. View is to the west at the main facade.

On front of card: &quot;Cassey, Photographer, Lansing, Mich.&quot; &quot;Views of the Capitol of Michigan, And Lansing and Vicinity.&quot;

Date not identified. Circa 1880.

A view of the Michigan State Capitol building. View is to the west at the main facade. Trees and street lights are visible.

On front of card: &quot;Cassey &amp; Whitney, Photographers, Lansing, Mich.&quot; &quot;Central Michigan Art Gallery.&quot;

Date not identified.

A view of the Michigan State Capitol building. View is to the west at the main facade. Pedestrians, trees, and street lights are visible.

On front right photo: &quot;3101. State Capitol, Front View, Lansing, Mich.&quot;

Stamped in purple ink on back: &quot;Geo. W. Sampson.&quot;

Date and photographer not identified.

A view of the Michigan State Capitol building. View is to the west at the main facade. Pedestrians, trees, and street lights are visible.

On front right photo: &quot;3101. State Capitol, Front View, Lansing, Mich.&quot;

Date and photographer not identified.

A view of the Michigan State Capitol building. View is to the west at the main facade. Pedestrians, trees, and street lights are visible.

On front right photo: &quot;3101. State Capitol, Front View, Lansing, Mich.&quot; &quot;Webster &amp; Albee, Publishers, Rochester, N. Y.&quot; &quot;American Scenery.&quot;

Date not identified.

A view of the Michigan State Capitol building looking toward the front facade to the southwest. The image has been hand-tinted. Three boys, a man, and a horse-drawn carriage are visible.

On front right image: &quot;3102. State Capitol, from South-west, Lansing, Mich.&quot;

Photographer and date not identified.

A view of the Michigan State Capitol building looking toward the front facade to the southeast. The image has been slightly hand-tinted. Several trees are visible.

On front right image: &quot;3103. State Capitol, from North-west, Lansing, Mich.&quot; &quot;American Scenery.&quot;

Photographer and date not identified.

A view of the Michigan State Capitol building looking toward the front facade to the southeast. The image has been slightly hand-tinted. Several trees are visible.

On front right image: &quot;3103. State Capitol, from North-west, Lansing, Mich.&quot; &quot;American Scenery.&quot;

Photographer and date not identified.

A view of the Michigan State Capitol building looking toward the front facade to the west. Several pedestrians are visible.

On front of card: &quot;B&quot; Series. &quot;3101. State Capitol, Front View, Lansing, Mich.&quot;

Photographer and date not identified.

&quot;Metcalfe Canning Co Deseronto Ont.&quot;

Buildings with boxes of vegetables awaiting processing.

Hand-written text reads &quot;1200 bu on platform this morning. Run about 800 cases a day&quot;

One of a series of reproduction postcards of Deseronto, Ontario.

Formerly part of a collection belonging to William Carveth, and donated by Wendy Carveth in June 2024.

&quot;Chamber of Commerce Jan. 31/48&quot;

Firefighters tackling a fire at the Corby/Jamieson Bone building south of City Hall in Belleville [157-163 Front Street].

One of a series of photographs relating to firefighting in Belleville, Ontario, collected by the Vance family.

&quot;United Empire Loyalist Monument Belleville Canada 7952&quot; 

From a fold-out wallet of postcards produced by the Bulman Brothers.

One of a series of photographs donated via Orland French in May 2023.

&quot;7948 The Armouries Belleville Canada&quot;

View of the Armouries building at Bridge and Pinnacle streets in Belleville, Ontario. Railway tracks can be seen on Pinnacle Street. From a fold-out wallet of postcards produced by the Bulman Brothers.

One of a series of photographs donated via Orland French in May 2023.

&quot;Court House Belleville Canada 7450&quot;

Cars outside the Hastings County courthouse building on Pinnacle Street in Belleville, Ontario. From a fold-out wallet of postcards produced by the Bulman Brothers.

One of a series of photographs donated via Orland French in May 2023.

&quot;7946 Tabernacle United Church Belleville Ont.&quot;

From a fold-out wallet of postcards produced by the Bulman Brothers.

One of a series of photographs donated via Orland French in May 2023.

&quot;Monument to British Empire Loyalists, looking up Main Street. Belleville, Ontario. - 10&quot;

Front Street, Belleville, looking north from Dundas Street toward City Hall with the United Empire Loyalist memorial on the right. The postcard was published by the Photogelatine Engraving Company, Ottawa (PECO) [in operation from 1910-1953].

One of a series of photographs donated via Orland French in May 2023.

House at 181 Moira Street West, Belleville, Ontario, photographed for the Historic Structures Committee of the Hastings County Historical Society. The building was damaged by fire in March 1977 and later demolished.

Text on the rear of this photograph reads: &quot;Mrs Winters said built by a farmer for his daughter who liked to paint - the tower like structure on the front was where she painted - shutters added at a later date.&quot;

Photograph from Elaine Preston donated by Lois Foster in March 2021.

&quot;West Moira Street - Finkle Apts&quot;

Photograph taken by Garry Sherman of row houses at 52-56 Moira Street West, Belleville, Ontario for the Historic Structures Committee of the Hastings County Historical Society.

Some of the windows in the houses are broken.

&quot;Belleville lower bridge opening ceremony&quot;
Bridge on Bridge Street in Belleville, Ontario, with scaffolding visible under the structure, and people gathered on the bridge.

Part of <a href="https://discover.cabhc.ca/lazier-family-photographs-2" rel="noreferrer nofollow">a collection of Lazier family photographs</a> donated by Robert Lazier in September 2021.

&quot;Arnott's skating rink on Turnbull Street&quot; 
This building was actually <a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/cabhc/35680994700/">on Church Street</a>, just south of Dundas Street, in Belleville, Ontario. It was moved to Turnbull Street in 1911.

Part of <a href="https://discover.cabhc.ca/lazier-family-photographs-2" rel="noreferrer nofollow">a collection of Lazier family photographs</a> donated by Robert Lazier in September 2021.

&quot;Picture of Tabernacle Church in winter Time&quot;

Tabernacle Methodist Church on Church Street in Belleville, Ontario. Railway tracks in foreground.  There is a two storey house near the church and a water tower behind it.

One of a series of photographs in the John Fairbairn Anderson Collection, early 1900s.

Transcribed from slide: &quot;Belleville / City Hall&quot;

Photograph of Belleville City Hall, Belleville, Ontario, taken at night.

One of a series of <a href="https://discover.cabhc.ca/slides-of-belleville-and-ontario-scenes" rel="noreferrer nofollow">photographic slides donated by Rudolf Donninger</a> in April 2021.

&quot;St. Vincent de Paul [Roman Catholic] Church and Presbytery, Desronto, Ont.&quot;

One of a collection of postcards of Deseronto, Ontario, sent to Dora Mater of Winger, Ontario.

Donated by Rhoda Marr in August 2019.

People standing on the porch of the house at 358 Arthur Street in Tweed, Ontario. The Tweed Feed Mill building is visible in the background.

Taken by Thomas Donoahue of Tweed. Possibly originally donated in 2010 as part of <a href="https://www.flickr.com/search/?user_id=134017397@N03&amp;sort=date-taken-desc&amp;text=2010-47&amp;view_all=1">2010-47</a> from the Prince Edward Historical Society.

Information from Evan Morton of Tweed: &quot;The house was owned by Robert &amp; Lucy (Prentice) Maguire/McGuire from 1907 Dec. 03 until his death 1923 May 22, then by his wife Lucy until she sold it to their only son Charles Ralph, 1933 Oct. 02. He sold the property 1943 Sept. 24 to Annie Doran. Charles Ralph Maguire married Ida Christina Sager, daughter of William &amp; Catherine (Wilson) Sager, at Tweed, 1914 Oct. 21. Their only child, a son, Colin Ross was born at Tweed 1915 Aug. 17. This photo may have been taken in the spring of 1916. I would suggest that the people are: (left to right) Charles Ralph Maguire, his wife Christina (Sager) with their son Colin Ross, Stella Sager ( Christina’s younger sister, by 2 years), Lucy (Prentice) Maguire, Robert Maguire, and Gordon or Frank Sager (brothers of Christina, younger by 4 or 6 years). According to Frank’s obituary (The Tweed News, 1964 May 22), he was the youngest of 11 children. &quot;

&quot;Pinnacle St. Belleville, 1896&quot;

Locomotive #630, coal tender and freight cars, in the snow on Pinnacle Street, Belleville, Ontario. The photo was taken in front of the Market Square and the Police Station building.

Photograph donated to Glanmore National Historic Site in 2003 by John Marsh. Transferred to the Community Archives in November 2019.

On back: &quot;# 16 Former movie theatre on south end of Front St., Belleville, ON&quot;

Photograph of property at 139 Front Street, Belleville, Ontario, formerly the Capitol and Park Theatre.

Part of the collection of the Quinte Arts Council of Belleville, Ontario

&quot;64 South Front Street&quot;

A drawing of an old building that was located on South Front Street overlooking Victoria Harbour in Belleville, Ontario.  The building was razed in the 1980s and replaced with a row of 8 townhouses.
This artwork was featured in the Belleville Art Association's &quot;Belleville Today&quot; calendar, compiled in 1977 for the City's centennial in 1978.  This image was drawn by artist, Poul Thrane.

&quot;Front Street, Madoc, Ont.&quot;
Postcard of Durham Street, Madoc, Ontario, looking north at its junction with St. Lawrence Street.  The spire of St. John's church is visible in the background.

Donated by Norma Lentz in November 2017.

&quot;Bay View, Trenton, Ont.&quot;
Postcard of the view from Mount Pelion in Trenton, Ontario, across the Bay of Quinte, with some of the buildings of downtown Trenton, and two churches visible.  industrial buildings line both shores of the Trent River.  The tower of the Post Office is visible in the centre.

Donated by Norma Lentz in November 2017.

&quot;Trenton High School, Trenton, Ont.&quot;
Postcard of the high school on College Street in Trenton, Ontario,showing staff and students outside the building. 

Donated by Norma Lentz in November 2017.

&quot;Dominion Entomological Building, Belleville, Ont.-38&quot;

Tinted postcard showing the former Carman property at 228 Dundas Street, Belleville, Ontario.  When this building was torn down to build a newer Dominion Entomological facility, the fountain was installed in front of Glanmore, where it stayed for several years. It was moved to the Moodie Cottage in 1997 and dismantled for storage in 2019.
When no longer used as a lab the building was used for federal departments, later became a retirement home and closed.  The brick has been covered with stucco.

&quot;MAIN BLDGS., BELLEVILLE GENERAL HOSPITAL B-11&quot;
located on Dundas Street East near MacDonald Avenue in Belleville, Ontario.  This building was later replaced by a six storey hospital in the mid 1970s, and a further extension in the early 2000s  The earliest building has been demolished.
Part of a set of fold-out postcards of Belleville, Ontario. Manufactured by Valentine-Black Ltd, of Toronto.

&quot;HIGH SCHOOL, BELLEVILLE, ONTARIO B-13&quot;
This card shows the Belleville Collegiate &amp; Vocational Institute rather than the earlier Belleville High School.  Plans in the early 1990s to convert the vacant building into a library and cultural centre were unsuccessful and the building was demolished.

Part of a set of fold-out postcards of Belleville, Ontario. Manufactured by Valentine-Black Ltd, of Toronto, showing the Belleville Collegiate Institute and Vocational School.

&quot;ALBERT COLLEGE, BELLEVILLE, ONTARIO B-14&quot;
located on the northeast corner of Dundas Street West and Sidney Street in Belleville, Ontario.
Part of a set of fold-out postcards of Belleville, Ontario. Manufactured by Valentine-Black Ltd, of Toronto.

&quot;CLUB HOUSE, QUINTE GOLF AND COUNTRY CLUB, BELLEVILLE, ONT., CANADA B 17&quot;
This building on the shores of the Bay of Quinte, burned and was replaced by a modern concrete clubhouse circa 1970, which was levelled circa 2015 and replaced with another clubhouse, this time on the north side of the #2 highway.
Part of a set of fold-out postcards of Belleville, Ontario. Manufactured by Valentine-Black Ltd, of Toronto.

&quot;POST OFFICE, QUINTE HOTEL, CHURCHES, ARMOURIES, BELLEVILLE, ONTARIO B-2&quot;

Photo likely taken from the City Hall tower looking northeast.
Part of a set of fold-out postcards of Belleville, Ontario.   The towers of both Bridge Street United Church and St. Thomas Anglican Church are visible. Also visible the Armouries and Quinte Hotel facing each other across Bridge Street. Manufactured by Valentine-Black Ltd, of Toronto.

&quot;CHURCH OF ST. MICHAEL THE ARCHANGEL (R.C.) BELLEVILLE, ONTARIO, CANADA B-3&quot;
on the west side of Church Street, north of Victoria Avenue in Belleville,  Ontario.
Part of a set of fold-out postcards of Belleville, Ontario. Manufactured by Valentine-Black Ltd, of Toronto.

&quot;ST. THOMAS' CHURCH (ANGLICAN) BELLEVILLE, ONTARIO B-4&quot;
on the northeast corner of Bridge and Church Streets in Belleville, Ontario.
Part of a set of fold-out postcards of Belleville, Ontario. Manufactured by Valentine-Black Ltd, of Toronto.  Taken from the southwest corner of Bridge and Church Streets looking northeast.  This building was gutted by fire in April 1974,  Much of the exterior was retained, but the interior modern building and roof are now held up by massive concrete pillars in the sanctuary.  A modern section was added to house the parish hall activities in the same building rather than across Bridge Street.  Once the congregation was able to use the new church building, the Parish Hall was subsequently sold in the 1980's and converted into the Bridge Street Retirement Residence.

&quot;BRIDGE STREET UNITED CHURCH, BELLEVILLE, ONTARIO B-5&quot;
This photo was taken from the lawn of St. Thomas Anglican Church on the east side of Church Street.
Part of a set of fold-out postcards of Belleville, Ontario. Manufactured by Valentine-Black Ltd, of Toronto.  Taken from the lawn of St. Thomas Anglican Church across Church Street.

&quot;WAR MEMORIAL, BELLEVILLE, ONT., CANADA B 19&quot;

Part of a set of fold-out postcards of Belleville, Ontario. Manufactured by Valentine-Black Ltd, of Toronto.
Is this monument still in existence as part of the cenotaph on Station Street.  There  seem to be housed in the background and to the left of the monument what looks like a ploughed field.

&quot;CHRISTCHURCH, NEW ZEALAND, Jan 20, 1909&quot;
Photograph of the Victoria Clock Tower in Christchurch, New Zealand.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;Street Scene, Belleville, Ont.&quot;
Reproduction of postcard showing Front Street in Belleville, Ontario, looking south, with Cherry Press on the left, Geen's Drug Store on the right and City Hall in the distance.

Negative No. B-41

&quot;POST OFFICE, RANGOON&quot;
Ornate post office building in Yangon, Myanmar.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;EDINBURGH CASTLE&quot;
Pipe bands marching inside Edinburgh Castle, Scotland.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;EDINBURGH CASTLE&quot;
Two men standing on the Esplanade in front of Edinburgh Castle, Scotland.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;PALACES IN THE FORT, AGRA OCT 3RD 1909&quot;
Three people standing in the courtyard of the fort at Agra, India.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;PALACES IN THE FORT, AGRA OCT 3RD 1909&quot;
Man standing in the courtyard of the fort at Agra, India.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;A PROCESSION IN THE CATHOLIC CATHEDRAL COMPOUND, OCT 3RD 1909&quot;
Procession of women at the Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception, Agra, India.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;POST OFFICE - CALCUTTA'&quot;
Post Office building in Kolkata, India.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;THE CUBBON HOTEL, BANGALORE&quot;
A hotel building in Bangalore (Bengalaru), India. The Kilties were in Bengalaru on September 13th and 14th, 1909.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;ONE OF THE PALACES IN MYSORE&quot;
View of Bangalore Palace in Bangalore (Bengaluru), India.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;LEAVING SYDNEY FOR AUCKLAND, NOV. 11TH, 1908.&quot;
The quayside in Sydney, Australia, with people on the dock and the Union Steam Ship Company of New Zealand building in the background. On the hill in the distance is the Model Lodging House. One of the people on the wharf is identified as E. Shipman.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;CHRISTCHURCH, NEW ZEALAND, Jan 20, 1909&quot;
Photograph of the Victoria Clock Tower in Christchurch, New Zealand.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;CHRISTCHURCH, NEW ZEALAND, Jan 20, 1909&quot;
Aerial view of Christchurch, New Zealand [possibly from Lichfield Street, looking east].

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;CHRISTCHURCH, NEW ZEALAND, Jan 20, 1909&quot;
View looking west along Lichfield Street and including the Victoria Clock Tower and a piano and organ store in Christchurch, New Zealand.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;STREET SCENE, CHRISTCHURCH, NEW ZEALAND, 12-7-08&quot;
Photograph of buildings around the Victoria Clock Tower in Christchurch, New Zealand. There is an electric streetcar and M. J. Weeks, Printers, in the centre of the picture, Wallace &amp; Co. Chemists to the left, and Milner &amp; Thompson pianos and a store selling Edison phonographs and records to the right.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;CHRISTCHURCH, NEW ZEALAND, Jan 20, 1909&quot;
Photograph of the Victoria Clock Tower in Christchurch, New Zealand.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;'BIRDSEED' HOTEL, CHARTERS TOWERS QUE. SEPT. 23, 1908&quot;
Photograph of the White Horse Hotel in Charters Towers, Queensland, Australia, with members of the Kilties band and other people standing under a sign reading &quot;J. Benham&quot;.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;THE JAIL, GLADSTONE, S. AUS 5-20-1909&quot;
Photograph of members of the Kilties band next to a stone building and wall [Gladstone Gaol, South Australia].

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;TOWNSVILLE, QUE. 9-20-1908&quot;
Photograph of Castle Hill in Townsville, Queensland, Australia. A building on the left carries the words &quot;HOPKINS &amp; Co. LTD&quot; [business premises of Hollis Hopkins].

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;A GRAIN 'ELEVATOR', BRINKWORTH, SOUTH AUS.&quot;
Sacks piled up in an open-sided building.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;EIGHT-HORSE TEAM, GLADSTONE, S. AUS 5-20-09&quot;
&quot;A REAL LOAD&quot;
Two eight horse teams standing in opposite directions, one pulling a tall load of hay.  Both loads are on carts standing outside a store in Gladstone, South Australia.  The sign above the store on two sides of the building may say &quot;James &amp; Co.&quot;  &amp; &quot;We sell...brand&quot;

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;THE THREE 'GRACES', VICTORIA, B.C. 7-17-08&quot;
Three women standing in front of a stone building with arched windows on the upper floor. The woman on the right of the image is Lena Power.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;CHURCH PARADE, BELLEVILLE, MAY 24 1908&quot;
A uniformed marching band on Bridge Street, Belleville, Ontario.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

Stereoscopic view of the Griffin Opera House stage, 65 Bridge Street on the southwest corner of Bridge and Church streets in Belleville, Ontario. Circa 1900
Handwritten text on the side indicates &quot;Opera House&quot;

Negative No. B-1

&quot;Old iron works, Deseronto&quot;

Photograph of the iron works building in Deseronto, Ontario, with snow on the ground.

Donated by Dolores Parks, November 1998.

&quot;Funeral of Dr. Oronhyatekha All Saints Church Tyendinaga Reserve&quot;

Photograph of the coffin of Dr Oronhyatekha on its way into All Saints Church, Tyendinaga, Ontario.

Scanned image donated by Joy Brant, July 2011.

Front page of program for Deseronto High School's Commencement of November 25, 1958. The event was held at the Bayview (Naylor's) Theatre, Deseronto, Ontario.

&quot;Annual Commencement Exercises of Deseronto High School. Guest speaker: Mr. D. McCormack Smyth, Assistant Registrar, University of Toronto, Bayview Theatre - Deseronto, Tuesday, November Twenty-Fifth, Nineteen Hundred and Fifty-Eight at 7.30 p.m.&quot;

Item donated by Bev Boomhour, November 2010.

&quot;Municipal Offices, Deseronto, Ontario&quot;

Photo-postcard of the Town Hall, Main Street, Deseronto, Ontario. The postcard was printed by the Photogelatine Engraving Co. Limited, Toronto which was in existence from 1910 to 1953.

Part of a collection of Deseronto-related materials donated by Ealaine Lawlor, December 2010.

&quot;Old Greek Church, Winnipeg&quot;

Photograph of a church in Winnipeg, Manitoba, taken in 1907.

Thanks to information from Flickr user ctomarctus, this has been identified as  the Ukrainian Orthodox Church known as &quot;The Tin Can Cathedral&quot; on King Street and Stella Avenue.

&quot;84 C.T.S. Oh! These Cadets! Capt Vernon Castle in foreground, Canada 1917&quot;

Hangars visible in background behind crashed aircraft.

Photo from an album that belonged to Christopher Paulus Devos, a sergeant in the Royal Flying Corps who was an instructor for 84 Canadian Training Squadron, based at Camp Mohawk, near Deseronto, Ontario.

Ging's, a well-known theatrical costumier and fancy dress suppliers on Dame Street in Dublin. The closed down shop of Maurice Abrahams, Master Bespoke Tailor, is festooned with posters for Zero's Disco which also adorned our <a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/nlireland/6536658801/" target="_blank"><b>Upper Ormond Quay pissoir</b></a> in another Michael S. Walker photo from 1973. Thanks to <b><a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/73371644@N04/" target="_blank">Slow Loose Chippings</a></b> for letting us know that &quot;<i>Zero's Disco was in Wolfe Tone St. &quot;Brush&quot; Sheils used to play there, amongst others!</i>&quot;

Here's the current <a href="http://maps.google.com/maps?q=dame+street,+dublin,+ireland&amp;hl=en&amp;ll=53.344159,-6.26667&amp;spn=0.01085,0.028539&amp;sll=37.0625,-95.677068&amp;sspn=58.076329,116.894531&amp;hnear=Dame+St,+Dublin,+County+Dublin+City,+Ireland&amp;t=m&amp;z=16&amp;layer=c&amp;cbll=53.344156,-6.266815&amp;panoid=EsY_3azT_5PRLszfp0Qxtg&amp;cbp=12,147.94,,0,5.44" target="_blank" rel="nofollow"><b>Street View</b></a>.

Thanks to <b>Niall McAuley</b> for a gorgeous photo of this location, including Ging's (from <b><a href="http://www.dublin.ie/forums/attachment.php?s=94b7d40ab312d5c889252071a882affb&amp;attachmentid=86165&amp;d=1320227483" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">dublin dot ie forums</a></b>).

<b>lkatadmirer</b> said that &quot;<i>Maurice Abrahams is now located in South Anne Street. The business is run by Mr Abrahams's son Declan and it is one of the best mens shops in Dublin.</i>&quot;

And very special thanks to <b>decospiper</b> for this lovely information:
&quot;<i>Katie Abrahams who is Declans daughter and Maurice's grandaughter found this photo today of our first 'proper retail' shop(very first shop was in Mary Street in 1962)The photo was taken when a compulsory purchase order by the council meant the whole block was to be knocked down to make way for the forecourt we now know beside Dublin Castle.And so,we progressed to 11 Parliament Street where we traded for about 20 years also opening our flagship Sth Anne Street shop where we still trade.Thanks to everyone here for finding this great photo for us!</i>&quot;

Date: 1973

NLI Ref.: <a href="http://catalogue.nli.ie/Record/vtls000269410" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">WALK121</a>

The late lamented Theatre Royal on Hawkins Street in Dublin.

Thanks to <b><a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/swordscookie/" target="_blank">swordscookie</a></b> for this information on the monument in the foreground:
&quot;<i>Monument to Constable Jeremiah Sheehan, 6' 4&quot; tall of the DMP who went down into a drain at this point to rescue some council workmen who had been overcome with sewer gas and lost his own life!</i>&quot;

<b><a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/gnmcauley/" target="_blank">Niall McAuley</a></b> contributed <a href="http://www.arthurlloyd.co.uk/Dublin/TheatreRoyalDublin/TheatreRoyalDublin.htm" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">this brilliant link</a> outlining the long history of the Theatre Royal &quot;<i>including the programme of the final show, the &quot;Royale Finale&quot;, which was on Saturday 30th of June at 8pm, just three months after this photo.</i>&quot;

Date: Sunday, 1 April 1962

NLI Ref.: <a href="http://catalogue.nli.ie/Record/vtls000269499" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">WALK127</a>

This is the Balloon House, and the photo was taken at Drumlargan, Co. Meath.

We asked to know more about this interesting house, and we certainly got exactly what we asked for...

Our excellent <b><a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/swordscookie/" target="_blank">swordscookie</a></b> did a Google search and found this account in a book about the Bomford family estates:
&quot;<i>A farcical error took place about the middle of the 1800s concerning two little houses on the Drumlargan road belonging to Lord Langford. Lord Langford of Summerhill designed a straight avenue to run one mile from Lynch’s Knock beside his house onto the road at Drumlargan. An architect was asked to design two gate-lodges to be placed at the end of the avenue on either side of the main road; the same architect also had to design two block-houses for the Indian Army to be sited to guard the Khyber Pass leading into Afghanistan. Needless to say the plans got mixed up and two low square block- houses with roofs like balloon-shaped pyramids were built on the road at Drumlargan. It would be interesting to know what the Indian Army thought about their blockhouses, but the Irish were intrigued with their “Balloon Houses” as they were soon named, and they remained an outstanding piece of local architecture until they were unjustifiably pulled down quite recently.</i>&quot;

Great information in from <b>Geri 65</b> who has a lovely family connection with this fantastic house:
&quot;<i>My grand parents lived in balloon house, summerhill. My mum and her brothers and sisters all lived in this house. My mum spoke about balloon house with fond memories. My mum was born 1923 and am certain she was born in this house. I have a copy of my grandmothers will regarding the sale of balloon house. My mum passed away 6 years ago and I know she would be over the moon that people have took an interest in this beautiful house which she lived in and grew up in. Hope this has helped.</i>&quot;

And we're delighted that <b>Geri 65</b> came back with some extra stories about her Mum, who would have been around 14 when this photo was taken, and about the Reilly family's life at the Balloon House in Drumlargan:
&quot;<i>Hello Everyone. Thank you all for taken an interest in Balloon House. I do not have a great deal of info to tell you sadly. As my mum and her brothers and sisters have passed away. My grandparents name was a good Irish name. The Reilly Family. There where 6 children in the family. So 8 people lived in this lovely house. The house i believe was very basic inside and not very big at all. But as in all old Irish homes, my mum mentioned a huge fireplace with a wonderful fire burning. Keepin the house warm and there was always pots boiling on it. Even through the summer months. My grandparents had a small farm on the land around the house. I noticed in the photo , what I think is a old bike against the side wall. (well it looks like a bike). I can remember my mum mentioning the bike. Which was used as there form of transport. If this picture was taken in 1937, then my mum would have been 14 years old and working for a family, who she called &quot;Gentry&quot;. I think it was an English family who had a stud farm for horses. I wonder if this bike,is the one my mum used to get to work. Ha ha. Lovely thought. I know the house was built on a main road because I can remember my mum telling me that nobody could pass the house without my granny knowing about it. That always made me smile. I will dig out the Will of my grandmother and try find out more. May take some time, as my loft is full. What I do know is that mum loved Balloon House and she would be delighted for your interest. Thank you all for making me smile. Just to let you know, so glad I found this site. Hopefully soon I will have some more info. God Bless x</i>&quot;

Date: 1937

